### What does this question ask?

The interviewer wants you to format dates using the java.time API. This tests DateTimeFormatter pattern syntax, locale awareness, and stream integration.

### How do you format joining dates in a stream?

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter;
import java.util.*;
import java.util.Locale;

public class FormatJoinDate {
    private static final DateTimeFormatter FORMATTER =
        DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("dd-MMM-yyyy", Locale.ENGLISH);

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> employees = Employee.getSampleEmployees();

        employees.stream()
            .sorted(Comparator.comparing(Employee::getJoinDate))
            .forEach(e -> System.out.println(
                e.getName() + ": " + e.getJoinDate().format(FORMATTER)));
    }
}
```

```text
Suresh: 01-Apr-2005
Rajesh: 01-Mar-2015
Amit: 10-Sep-2018
Kavita: 01-Jul-2019
Priya: 01-Jun-2021
Neha: 15-Mar-2022
Ananya: 10-Jan-2023
Meera: 20-Jul-2024
```

### What do the pattern letters mean?

| Pattern | Meaning | Example |
|---|---|---|
| dd | Day of month (2 digits, zero-padded) | 01, 15, 31 |
| d | Day of month (1-2 digits) | 1, 15, 31 |
| MMM | Month abbreviation (3 letters) | Jan, Feb, Dec |
| MM | Month number (2 digits) | 01, 06, 12 |
| MMMM | Full month name | January, June |
| yyyy | Year (4 digits) | 2021, 2024 |
| yy | Year (2 digits) | 21, 24 |
| EEEE | Full day name | Monday, Friday |
| EEE | Day abbreviation | Mon, Fri |

```java
// Different patterns
LocalDate date = LocalDate.of(2021, 6, 15);

System.out.println(date.format(DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("dd/MM/yyyy")));
// 15/06/2021

System.out.println(date.format(DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("MMMM dd, yyyy", Locale.ENGLISH)));
// June 15, 2021

System.out.println(date.format(DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("EEE, dd MMM yyyy", Locale.ENGLISH)));
// Tue, 15 Jun 2021
```

### Why is Locale important?

Without specifying a locale, MMM uses the system default locale, which could produce month names in Hindi, Japanese, or any other language.

```java
// WRONG: depends on system locale
DateTimeFormatter wrong = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("dd-MMM-yyyy");
// On a Hindi system: 15-जून-2021

// CORRECT: explicit English locale
DateTimeFormatter correct = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern(
    "dd-MMM-yyyy", Locale.ENGLISH);
// Always: 15-Jun-2021
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake 1: using mm instead of MM for months.

```java
// WRONG: mm is minutes, not months!
DateTimeFormatter wrong = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("dd-mm-yyyy");
// Throws DateTimeException: "Field MinuteOfHour cannot be printed"

// CORRECT: MM is months
DateTimeFormatter correct = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("dd-MM-yyyy");
```

Mistake 2: creating a new formatter inside the stream for every element.

```java
// WRONG: creates a new formatter for every employee
employees.stream()
    .map(e -> e.getJoinDate().format(
        DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("dd-MMM-yyyy")))  // new formatter each time!
    .toList();

// CORRECT: reuse a static final formatter
private static final DateTimeFormatter FMT =
    DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("dd-MMM-yyyy", Locale.ENGLISH);
employees.stream()
    .map(e -> e.getJoinDate().format(FMT))
    .toList();
```

### How do you parse a formatted string back to LocalDate?

```java
String dateStr = "15-Jun-2021";
LocalDate parsed = LocalDate.parse(dateStr,
    DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("dd-MMM-yyyy", Locale.ENGLISH));
System.out.println(parsed);  // 2021-06-15
```

### How do you build a formatted report with streams?

```java
String report = employees.stream()
    .sorted(Comparator.comparing(Employee::getJoinDate))
    .map(e -> String.format("%-10s %-12s %s",
        e.getName(),
        e.getDepartment(),
        e.getJoinDate().format(FORMATTER)))
    .collect(Collectors.joining("\n"));

System.out.println(report);
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you format with time?" Use `DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("dd-MMM-yyyy HH:mm:ss")` with `LocalDateTime`.
- "Is DateTimeFormatter thread-safe?" Yes, it is immutable and safe for concurrent use.
- "How do you handle invalid date strings during parse?" Catch `DateTimeParseException`.
